      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "The objective of our work is to study the clinical features and the management of pulmonary embolism in a cardiology department."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIAL AND METHODS",
          "content": "It is a retrospective study including 43 patients between 1993 and 2003 in the cardiology department of a Tunisian hospital for acute pulmonary embolism."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The most common clinical signs were dyspnea, pleural pain and deep venous thrombosis. The most common physical signs were tachypnea and tachycardia. Electrocardiographic abnormalities were found in 38 patients (88.4%). Chest X-ray was abnormal in 25 patients (60.5%). The arterial blood gas found hypoxia and\\or hypocapnia in 95% of cases. D-dimer was high in almost totality of patients (96%). US doppler of deep leg veins was positive in more than 40% of patients. Transthoracic echocardiography was normal in more than 40% of the patients. Lung scan noted a high or very high probability in 87% of cases. Pulmonary helical CT was positive in majority of cases. Unfractionated heparin was administered to about 75% of patients and low-molecular-weight-heparin was prescribed at only 40% of patients."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Management of pulmonary embolism in our cardiology department is characterized by the frequent use of non invasive techniques on the diagnostic view and appeal to heparine on the therapeutic view."
        }
      ]},
